  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nScattered showers will continue to swing across the region \nthrough 09Z, mainly north of I-70, with no significant impact. \nPrecipitation potential past sunrise and into this evening will \nbe largely squashed by the presence of flat mid-level ridging \nand surface high pressure over our region. The next shortwave \nriding in the northwest flow over the southeast CONUS ridge will\narrive tonight, with additional disturbances through Friday. \nGiven the shortwaves riding over a low-level warm advection \npattern with a nearly stationary effective boundary to our \nsouthwest, there are indications that training thunderstorms \nproducing heavy rain and flash flood risks will once again be a \nthreat somewhere in the Ohio Valley.\n\nRight now, hi-res ensemble guidance indicates that such an axis\nis most likely to set up to the southwest of our region.\nThe 00Z HREF/REFS both keep the axis of highest max potential rain\ntotals through 00Z Saturday back towards the Middle Ohio Valley\nand into Huntington WV. That said, there are still a couple hi-\nres model scenarios (in particular the NAM) that bring higher \nrain totals as far north as the Mason-Dixon Line, which could \nthreaten still- vulnerable areas in eastern Ohio. While the \nmost-likely scenario appears to keep the main flooding threat \nout of our area, continued vigilance is merited given the \nrelatively poor model performance with thunderstorm complexes in\nthis pattern. Mesoscale details, most of which will be \ninfluenced by future convective activity, will play a large role\nin how things play out, and as such, overall confidence in the \noutcome is relatively low. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe synoptic pattern will undergo little change through the \ncoming days. The predominant flow setup (ridge south to \nsouthwest of region and zonal to northwest flow overhead) stays \nintact potentially through mid to late next week. Periodic \nshowers and thunderstorms, generally shorter in duration \ncompared to this past week, remain possible in such flow while \ntemperature largely stays near seasonal averages. Saturday is \nthe more likely day of break from activity as Eastern Canada \nsurface high pressure may build south, but even that scenario \ncould change as models attempt to better time shortwave \nmovement/timing.\n\nLonger-range machine learning guidance continues to suggest that\nboth severe and flash flood risks remain more limited over the\ncoming days than the previous several days. Still, it remains \nprudent to follow the latest forecast trends, as there are still\nscenarios that could bring us higher threats than currently\nindicated.",
